WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.159
- [Instructor] We are
told that the first five

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:06.830
ionization energies for
a third period element

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.970
are shown below.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.370
What is the identity of the element?

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.570
So pause this video

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.210
and see if you can
figure it out on your own

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.310
and it'll probably be handy

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.648
to have a periodic table of elements.

00:00:21.648 --> 00:00:24.910
So before I even look at a
periodic table of elements,

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:26.800
let's make sure we
understand what this table

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:27.890
is telling us.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.890
This is telling us that if
we start with a neutral atom

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:32.270
of this mystery element,

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:35.866
it would take 578 kilojoules per mole

00:00:35.866 --> 00:00:38.740
to remove that first electron

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.570
to turn that atom into an ion

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:45.480
with a plus one positive charge.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.480
And then, it would take another
1,817 kilojoules per mole

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.210
to remove a second electron.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.230
So to make that ion even more positive.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.192
And then after that it would take another

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:04.192
2,745 kilajoules per mole to
remove the third electron.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.240
And then to remove the fourth electron,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.780
it takes a way larger amount of energy.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.510
It takes 11,578 kilojoules per mole.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.740
And then the fifth
electron takes even more,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.350
14,842 kilojoules per mole.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.152
And so, for the first, second, and third

00:01:21.152 --> 00:01:24.120
you do have an increase
in ionization energy,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.530
but when you go to the
fourth the energy required

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.750
to remove those is way higher.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:33.750
So to me, these look like you're
removing valence electrons

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:39.150
and these look like you're
removing core electrons.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.830
So one way to think about it is

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.280
let's look on our
periodic table of elements

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.780
and look for a third period element

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.960
that has three valence electrons.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.320
So we have our periodic table of elements.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.260
We want a third period element,

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.260
so it's gonna be in this third row

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.722
and which of these has
three valence electrons?

00:01:58.722 --> 00:02:01.350
Well, sodium has one valence electron,

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.186
magnesium has two valence electrons,

00:02:03.186 --> 00:02:07.120
aluminum has three valence electrons.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.720
So one way to think about it is

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.920
that first electron, it's a
reasonable ionization energy.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.191
Then the second one, a little higher.

00:02:14.191 --> 00:02:16.954
Then the third, a little
bit higher than after that,

00:02:16.954 --> 00:02:20.910
but then the fourth, you're
starting to go into the core.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.110
You're going to have to take an electron

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.240
out of that full second energy shell,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.090
which takes a lot of energy.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:29.420
And so this is pretty clearly aluminum

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:30.663
that is being described.

